in NLM Catalog†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L209:   * cite3†Add to Search L210: \nL211: . 2017 May;18(5):583-593.\nL212: \nL213: doi: 10.1038/ni.3693. Epub 2017 Mar 6.\nL214: ### Authors\nL215: \nL216: cite11†Jan C Rieckmann ^{  cite27†1 }, cite13†Roger Geiger ^{  cite28†2 cite29†3 }, cite16†Daniel Hornburg ^{  cite27†1 }, cite17†Tobias Wolf ^{  cite28†2 cite29†3 }, cite18†Ksenya Kveler ^{  cite30†4 }, cite20†David Jarrossay ^{  cite28†2 }, cite21†Federica Sallusto ^{  cite28†2 }, cite22†Shai S Shen-Orr ^{  cite30†4 }, cite23†Antonio Lanzavecchia ^{  cite28†2 cite29†3 }, cite24†Matthias Mann ^{  cite31†5 }, cite26†Felix Meissner ^{  cite27†1 }\nL217: ### Affiliations\nL218:   * ^{1} Experimental Systems Immunology, Max Planck Institute of Biochemistry, Bayern, Germany.\nL219:   * ^{2} Institute for Research in Biomedicine, Università della Svizzera italiana, Bellinzona, Switzerland.\nL220:   * ^{3} Institute of Microbiology, ETH Zürich, Zürich, Switzerland.\nL221:   * ^{4} Department of Immunology, Faculty of Medicine, Technion-Israel Institute of Technology, Haifa, Israel.\nL222:   * ^{5} Department of Proteomics and Signal Transduction, Max Planck Institute of Biochemistry, Bayern, Germany.\nL223:   * PMID: 28263321\nL224:   * DOI: cite8†10.1038/ni.3693†doi.org L225: \nL226: Item in Clipboard\nL227: \nL228: [Button: Full text links ]Cite\nL229: \nL230: Display options\nL231: \nL232: Display options\nL233: \nL234: Format [Select]\nL235: ## Abstract\nL236: The immune system is unique in its dynamic interplay between numerous cell types. However, a system-wide view of how immune cells communicate to protect against disease has not yet been established. We applied high-resolution mass-spectrometry-based proteomics to characterize 28 primary human hematopoietic cell populations in steady and activated states at a depth of >10,000 proteins in total.\nL237: Protein copy numbers revealed a specialization of immune cells for ligand and receptor expression, thereby connecting distinct immune functions. By integrating total and secreted proteomes, we discovered fundamental intercellular communication structures and previously unknown connections between cell types.\nL238: Our publicly accessible (http://www.immprot.org/) proteomic resource provides a framework for the orchestration of cellular interplay and a reference for altered communication associated with pathology.\nL239: cite32†PubMed Disclaimer L240: ## Comment in\nL241: \nL242:   * cite33†Immune regulation: Immune cell social networks. L243: \nL244: Bird L. Bird L. Nat Rev Immunol. 2017 Mar 27;17(4):216. doi: 10.1038/nri.2017.31. Nat Rev Immunol. 2017. PMID: 28345632 No abstract available.\nL245: \nL246:   * cite34†The immune system as a social network. L247: \nL248: Bergthaler A, Menche J. Bergthaler A, et al. Nat Immunol. 2017 Apr 18;18(5):481-482. doi: 10.1038/ni.3727. Nat Immunol. 2017. PMID: 28418389 No abstract available.\nL249: ## References\nL250: \nL251:   1.     1. Nature. 2012 Sep 6;489(7414):91-100 - cite35†PubMed L252:   2.     1. Immunity. 2011 Aug 26;35(2):161-8 - cite36†PubMed L253:   3.     1. BMC Syst Biol. 2012;6 Suppl 2:S7 - cite37†PubMed L254:   4.     1. Database (Oxford). 2011 Mar 29;2011:bar009 - cite38†PubMed L255:   5.     1. Proteomics Clin Appl. 2012 Oct;6(9-10):497-501 - cite39†PubMed L256: \nL257: [Button: Show all 59 references ]\nL258: ## Publication types\nL259: \nL260:   * [Button: Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't ]\nL261: \nL262: Actions\nL263: \nL264:     * cite40†Search in PubMed L265:     * cite41†Search in MeSH†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L266:     * cite3†Add to Search L267: ## MeSH terms\nL268: \nL269:   * [Button: Animals ]\nL270: \nL271: Actions\nL272: \nL273:     * cite42†Search in PubMed L274:     * cite43†Search in MeSH†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L275:     * cite3†Add to Search L276: \nL277:   * [Button: Blood Cells / physiology* 